-- title:
--   Twice the decomposition order counts automorphisms of (E,Cyc), q=2
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ denotes `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, and for a field $K$ and a $K$-extension $F$ a *place* of $F/K$ is, as in the project, a valuation subring of $F$ containing the image of $K$, different from $F$, whose ideals are principal; `ord` is the associated normalised valuation and `evalAt` the residue taken back in $K$ when the place is rational.
--
--   **Base data.** A prime $q$ together with the hypothesis $hq2 : q = 2$; a non-zero natural number $M'$ with $q \nmid M'$; a prime $\ell_g$ with $\ell_g \equiv 11 \pmod{12}$ and $\ell_g \mid M'$; a valuation subring $A \subseteq \overline{\mathbb Q}$ with `A.LiesOverPrime q`, i.e. $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. Write $\kappa =$ `ResidueField A`.
--
--   **Supersingular place.** A finite set $W$ of places of `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` over $\kappa$ whose members are exactly the elements of `ssPlaces q M' κ`, the set of places satisfying the predicate `IsSupersingularPlace q M' κ` ($hW$), and a chosen element $s \in W$.
--
--   **Reduction datum.** The inclusion $hle$ of the base-changed full level-$M'$ modular function field `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` into `fieldBar q M'`; a constant reduction $R_0$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` with respect to $A$ and residue field `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` (a valuation subring `R₀.integers` of the big field, a surjective residue map onto `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` with kernel the maximal ideal, compatibility with $A$ and with constants, and a degree-preserving map of places compatible with orders); and $hR_0$, stating that every Laurent series $y$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise image lies in `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` belongs to `R₀.integers` and that its $R_0$-residue, read as a Laurent series over $\kappa$, is the coefficientwise reduction of $y$.
--
--   **Constants.** An intermediate field $k_0$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/\mathbb Q$ and $\pi_0 \in k_0$ lying in $A$; writing $A_0 = A \cap k_0$ (formally `A.comap (algebraMap ↥k₀ (AlgebraicClosure ℚ))`), the hypotheses that $A_0$ is a discrete valuation ring ($hdvr$) with maximal ideal generated by $\pi_0$ ($hunif$), henselian ($hhens$) and with algebraically closed residue field ($hres$), and $h\kappa$: every element of $A$ is congruent, modulo the maximal ideal of $A$, to an element of $k_0 \cap A$.
--
--   **Auxiliary prime and uniformisers.** A prime $\ell \ge 3$ with $\ell \ne q$ and $\ell \nmid M'$; an element $\zeta_0 \in k_0$ which is a primitive $q\ell$-th root of unity; an element $\varpi_t \in k_0$ lying in $A$ with $\varpi_t^{\,q^2-1} = q\,u$ for some unit $u$ of $A$ ($h\varpi t$). Further, an intermediate field $K_b$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}/k_0$ with $K_b = \bot$, a valuation subring $A_b \subseteq K_b$ cut out by $A$ ($hAb$), and a non-zero generator $\varpi_b$ of its maximal ideal. The tameness hypothesis $htame$ asserts that $(\ell_g-1)/2$ (natural-number division) is a unit of $A_0$, and $\xi \in k_0$ is a primitive $q$-th root of unity.
--
--   **Level and function field.** A subgroup $H_1 \le (\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times$ equal to `levelH q M'` (the kernel of reduction to $(\mathbb Z/q)^\times$-level data, namely $\ker$ of `ZMod.unitsMap (dvd_sq_mul q M')`) intersected with the kernel of reduction $(\mathbb Z/q^2M')^\times \to (\mathbb Z/\ell_g)^\times$ ($hH_1$); the intermediate field $K_\ell$ of `LaurentSeries ↥k₀` over $k_0$ equal to the Laurent base change to $k_0$ of `xHFunctionField (q ^ 2 * M') H₁` ($hK\ell$), together with the algebra and scalar-tower structures over $A_0$; and $j_\ell \in K_\ell$ whose underlying Laurent series is the image of `jq` under `coeffEmb ↥k₀`, with $j_\ell \ne 0$. Also an element $\varpi_t' \in A_0$ with image $\varpi_t$ in $k_0$.
--
--   **The chart and the point $y$.** `chartAlgFin A₀ K_ℓ j_ℓ` is the $A_0$-subalgebra of elements of $K_\ell$ integral over $A_0[j_\ell]$. The data include a maximal ideal $y$ of this algebra containing the image of $\pi_0$ ($h\varpi y$), and the supersingularity hypothesis $hss$: for every algebraically closed field $\Omega$ of characteristic $q$ and every ring homomorphism $\varphi$ from the chart algebra to $\Omega$ with kernel $y$, the value $\varphi(j_\ell)$ lies in `ssJSet q Ω`, the set of $j$ such that every elliptic curve over $\Omega$ with that $j$-invariant has no non-zero point killed by $q$.
--
--   The hypothesis $hover$ says that $y$ lies over $s$: for every $g$ in `modularFunctionFieldFull M'` whose base change lies in `R₀.integers`, which is regular at every place of `modularFunctionFieldBar M'` where $j$ is regular, and whose $R_0$-residue lies in the valuation subring of $s$, and for every constant $c \in k_0 \cap A$ whose residue in $\kappa$ equals the value at $s$ of that $R_0$-residue, the element $g$ of the chart algebra minus the image of $c$ lies in $y$.
--
--   The hypothesis $hOrbit$ says that $y$ is unique up to the level action: any maximal ideal $y'$ of the chart algebra containing $\pi_0$ and satisfying the same "lies over $s$" condition is obtained from $y$ by a level automorphism, i.e. there are $\gamma \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and $\tau \in \mathrm{Aut}_{k_0}(K_\ell)$ with `IsLevelAutAt ↥k₀ q ξ q (q ^ 2 * M') H₁ γ⁻¹ Kℓ τ` — the predicate saying that, for every embedding $\iota : k_0 \to \mathbb C$ with $\iota(\xi) = e^{2\pi i/q}$, $\tau$ transports a quotient of modular forms of level $\Gamma_{H_1}(q^2M')$ with integral $q$-expansions according to the slash action of the matrix `conjElemN q γ⁻¹` — such that $\tau$ preserves the chart algebra and $f \in y \iff \tau f \in y'$.
--
--   **The supersingular chart package.** An $A_0$-subalgebra $B_t \subseteq K_\ell$ and a valuation subring $W_t \subseteq K_\ell$ with $B_t \subseteq W_t$ ($hBW$), subject to the compound hypothesis $hS1$, whose clauses are summarised here: the chart algebra is contained in $B_t$ and $K_\ell$ is the fraction field of $B_t$; $B_t$ is formally smooth and of finite presentation over $A_0$ and $B_t/(\pi_0)$ has Krull dimension at most $1$; $W_t \cap k_0 = A_0$, the maximal ideal of $W_t$ is generated by $\pi_0$, $W_t$ is a discrete valuation ring, $y$ is the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_t$ to the chart algebra, and $W_t$ is the localisation of $B_t$ at that maximal ideal; for every $\mathbb F_{q^2}$-algebra structure (`GaloisField q 2`) on the residue field of $A_0$ there is a surjective ring homomorphism $\rho$ from $B_t$ onto [`DrinfeldCurve.CoordRing q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L21) of that residue field (the quotient of $\kappa_0[X_0,X_1]$ by `drinfeldIdeal`), with kernel the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_t$, compatible with $A_0 \to \kappa_0$, and equivariant for the level action: for each $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ and each level automorphism $\tau$ at $\gamma^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$ there is $c \in \mathbb F_{q^2}^\times$ with $(\mathrm{red}_q(\gamma), c)$ in [`DrinfeldCurve.hSubgroup q`](def/DrinfeldCurve_CoordRing.html#L276) (the kernel of $(g,c) \mapsto \det(g)\,c^{q+1}$) such that $\rho \circ \tau = \mathrm{hAction}(\mathrm{red}_q(\gamma),c) \circ \rho$ on $B_t$, with $c \ne 1$ whenever $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)$ and $\tau \ne \mathrm{id}$, and with the scalar $c$ also realised by some $\gamma' \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and level automorphism $\tau'$ preserving $W_t$ through the pair $(1,c)$ (the linked-scalars clause); finally $B_t$ is stable under level automorphisms at $\gamma^{-1}$ for $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$, every prime of $B_t$ containing $\pi_0$ is carried onto the contraction of the maximal ideal of $W_t$ by some level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, and an element of $B_t$ lying in the maximal ideal of $W_t$ after all such level automorphisms is divisible by $\pi_0$.
--
--   **The crossing data.** A natural number $n \ge 1$ dividing $q+1$; $\gamma_0 \in \Gamma(q) \cap \Gamma_0(M')$ and a level automorphism $\tau_0$ at $\gamma_0^{-1}$ preserving $W_t$; a natural number $m \ge 1$ with $\pi_0^m = \varpi_t' \cdot (\text{unit})$; an element $\zeta_c$ of the adic completion of $A_0$ with $\zeta_c^n = 1$ and $\zeta_c^k - 1$ a unit for $0 < k < n$; and a finite set `ends` of subrings of $K_\ell$. The compound hypothesis $hR7$ (summarised here) requires: $\tau_0^n$ acts trivially on $B_t$ while $\tau_0^k$ does not for $0 < k < n$, and every level automorphism with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$ agrees on $B_t$ with a power $\tau_0^k$, $k < n$; `ends` has exactly $q+1$ elements, is permuted by the level automorphisms from $\Gamma_0(M')$ preserving $W_t$, transitively so, each member being fixed by $\tau_0$, and distinct members are separated by an element that is a non-unit of one and a unit of the other; each $O \in \mathrm{ends}$ is contained in $W_t$, is a noetherian local ring containing the chart algebra, satisfies $O \cap k_0 = A_0$ and has residue field given by the constants, and carries a crossing presentation: elements $c_x, c_y \in O$ with $c_xc_y = \pi_0^m \cdot (\text{unit})$, an isomorphism $\iota$ of the adic completion of $O$ onto `UVCrossingModel` of the adic completion of $A_0$ at $\pi_0^m$ (the quotient of the two-variable power series ring by `uvCrossingIdeal`) matching constants and carrying $c_x, c_y$ to unit multiples of $U$ and $V$, with $c_y$ in and $c_x$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_t$, and with $\tau_0$ corresponding to an automorphism $\theta_0$ scaling $U$ by $\zeta_c$ and $V$ by the inverse $\zeta_c'$ modulo the square of the maximal ideal; together with a second branch valuation subring $W_x$ (with $O \subseteq W_x$, $W_x \cap k_0 = A_0$, $W_x$ a discrete valuation ring uniformised by $\pi_0$, a coordinate $t$ whose specialisations detect the maximal ideal of $A_0$, $c_x$ in and $c_y$ outside the maximal ideal of $W_x$, and the comparison of that maximal ideal with $y$ on the chart algebra), and a finite-type $A_0$-subalgebra $B_x \subseteq B_t \cap O$, stable under level automorphisms with $\gamma \in \Gamma(q)\cap\Gamma_0(M')$, consisting of functions regular wherever $j_\ell$ is, with $O$ the localisation of $B_x$; the last clause states that any valuation subring $V$ of $K_\ell$ with $V \cap k_0 = A_0$, containing the chart algebra and centred at $y$, either contains $B_t$ or dominates one of the members of `ends`.
--
--   The hypothesis $hR5d$ states that a level automorphism at $\gamma^{-1}$ with $\gamma \in \Gamma_0(M')$ which preserves $y$ on the chart algebra also preserves $W_t$.
--
--   **The moduli interpretation of $s$.** Finally, an elliptic Weierstrass curve $E$ over $\kappa =$ `ResidueField ↥A`, an additive subgroup $\mathrm{Cyc}$ of its affine points which is cyclic ($hcyc$) of cardinality $M'$ ($hcardCyc$), and the hypothesis $hP$ that the valuation subring of the place $s$ is the preimage, under the inclusion of `modularFunctionFieldC κ M'` into the full moduli function field, of the valuation subring of `moduliPlace κ M' E Cyc`.
--
--   **Conclusion.** Under these hypotheses,
--   $$2n = \#\{\iota : E(\kappa) \to E(\kappa) \text{ additive}\},$$
--   the cardinality being that of the set of additive endomorphisms $\iota$ of the group of affine points of $E$ such that: $\iota$ lies in [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalHomSet κ E E`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L28) (that is, $\iota = 0$ or $\iota$ is rationally represented); $\iota$ is invertible within that set, i.e. there is $\iota'$ in `rationalHomSet κ E E` with $\iota' \circ \iota = \mathrm{id}$ and $\iota \circ \iota' = \mathrm{id}$; and $\iota$ maps $\mathrm{Cyc}$ onto $\mathrm{Cyc}$. Note that the left-hand side carries the factor $2$ even though $q = 2$ here.
--
--   This is the order count on the Drinfeld component of the semistable chart above a supersingular point: it identifies twice the order $n$ of the cyclic group of level automorphisms acting on the chart with the number of automorphisms of the pair $(E, \mathrm{Cyc})$ attached to that point, in the $\Gamma_1(\ell_g)$ diamond frame at $q = 2$ with the scalar clause linked into the Drinfeld-quotient block. It is used by [`ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.rigidChart_decompositionOrder_eq_placeWidthChar_of_decompositionUnique_linkedScalars_of_eq_two_of_dvd`](thm.html#ModularCurve.FullLevel.Diamond.rigidChart_decompositionOrder_eq_placeWidthChar_of_decompositionUnique_linkedScalars_of_eq_two_of_dvd), where the decomposition order is converted into the width of the place, and is proved from the corresponding count of the decomposition group, its characterisation as the stabiliser of the point, and the comparison of that stabiliser with the automorphism group of the moduli pair.
